% -e over variants one of whose two plans would be estimated to do no
% work at all, which still keep both.  loop holds nothing.  The first
% variant's delta-first plan tests each delta tuple against loop and finds
% nothing; the second variant's input-first plan reads loop first and so
% reads nothing more.  An estimate is not a proof, and the sizes it is made
% from change from round to round, so each variant shows both of its plans
% under the rule a round chooses between them by.

e(a, b).
e(b, c).
e(c, d).

loop(X) :- e(X, X).

r(X, Y) :- e(X, Y).
r(a, Z) :- e(a, Y), r(Y, Z), loop(Z).
r(X, Z) :- loop(X), e(X, Y), r(Y, Z).
